rhaps they lack strong cards or are waiting.\n\nBut as landlord, I want to maintain control. Leading BlackJoker now asserts dominance and may win the trick outright if no one has RedJoker (but one player must).\n\nWait — there is 1 RedJoker unknown. So one of the peasants *does* have it. Therefore, if I play BlackJoker, the peasant with RedJoker will almost certainly play it and win. That means I lose the lead — very dangerous, because I\'m close to emptying my hand.\n\nTherefore, better to play a lower single and keep BlackJoker as an emergency escape or final move.\n\nBetween ♠10 and ♥K:  \n- ♥K is stronger than ♠10.  \n- Both are vulnerable to 2 and A, but more importantly, peasants may still hold A or 2 as singles.\n\nBut looking at recent plays:\n- Previous player played ♠3 ♥3 ♥8 ♣8 ♦8 — showing low triplets with attached singles, suggesting weaker hand.\n- Next player played ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♥9 ♣9 — triplet with a pair? No — wait, that’s three 7s and two 9s — actually invalid unless it was two separate plays? But per history, it\'s listed as one turn — likely a mistake, but assuming valid, maybe it was a full house type? But in Dou Dizhu, "full house" isn\'t legal — only combinations are defined.\n\nActually, rechecking: Turn 2 (Next Player): ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♥9 ♣9 — this is a triplet (7s) + a pair (9s), which is a valid **triplet with attached pair** — legal move.\n\nSo both peasants have used up some mid-level cards.\n\nNow, after that, both passed on my long sequence (Turn 3), which suggests they may not have long sequences or strong bombs.\n\nCurrent unknown cards include: A:2, K:3, Q:1, J:3, 10:2, etc.\n\nSo likely, neither peasant has many high singles left.\n\nTherefore, playing ♥K now might go unchallenged — or only beaten by A or 2 or joker.\n\nBut if I play ♠10, it\'s more likely to be beaten by A, K, Q, etc.\n\nSo best chance to retain lead is to play **♥K** — higher than 10, and peasants may prefer to save their A or 2 for later.\n\nEven though RedJoker exists, they won’t play it just to beat a King — too wasteful.\n\nSo ♥K is likely to win the trick, allowing me to continue next round.\n\nThen I can follow up with ♠10, then finally BlackJoker.\n\nBut if I play BlackJoker first, they counter with RedJoker, then I’m stuck with two cards while they gain control — very risky.\n\nHence, optimal strategy: play medium-high singles first to try to retain lead, save joker for last or emergency.\n\nTherefore, best move is **♥K**.\n\nReason: Playing ♥K offers a balance between asserting control and conserving the powerful BlackJoker for a future critical moment. It is high enough that peasants may choose to pass rather than waste an A, 2, or RedJoker, especially since they each still have 12 cards and likely want to preserve high cards. This increases the chance I retain the lead and can continue playing down my hand. Playing BlackJoker now would almost certainly draw out RedJoker, surrendering the lead — too risky at this stage.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥K"]}'}
